1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Welcome. Today we talk about how a machine learning model learns from data and why it sometimes fails on new data.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:30,000
A model is a function that maps inputs to outputs. We fit it by adjusting its parameters to reduce the error on training data.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Training data is what the model sees while learning. Test data is held back and used only to measure how well it generalizes.

4
00:00:45,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Generalization means performing well on examples the model has never seen. That is the real goal, not memorizing the training set.

5
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Overfitting happens when a model learns noise and tiny details of the training data. Training error is very low but test error is high.

6
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:30,000
A typical sign of overfitting is a big gap between training accuracy and test accuracy. Very complex models are most at risk.

7
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Underfitting is the opposite: the model is too simple to capture the pattern, so both training and test error are high.

8
00:01:45,000 --> 00:02:00,000
One way to fight overfitting is to get more training data, because it is harder to memorize a larger and more varied set.

9
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Another way is regularization, which adds a penalty for large parameter values so the model stays simpler.

10
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:30,000
L2 regularization, also called ridge, penalizes the sum of squared weights. It shrinks weights toward zero but rarely makes them exactly zero.

11
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:45,000
L1 regularization, also called lasso, penalizes the absolute value of weights. It can set some weights exactly to zero and so selects features.

12
00:02:45,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Cross-validation splits the training data into folds. We train on some folds and validate on the rest, then average the results.

13
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Cross-validation gives a more reliable estimate of performance and helps us choose settings like the regularization strength.

14
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:30,000
To summarize: aim for generalization, watch the gap between training and test error, and use regularization and cross-validation.
